## Document Map

| File | Answers | Updated |
|---|---|---|
| `PRODUCT_SPEC.md` | Current behavior, schema, canonical values | Every ship |
| `CHANGELOG.md` | Version history: the {{CHANGELOG_WINDOW}} most recent entries | Every ship; the oldest entry rolls into `changelog/` |
| `changelog/` | Sealed archives plus one open `v<first>-onward.md` | Rolls only; grep on demand |
| `ROADMAP.md` | Not-yet-built work only | Shrinks on ship; never gains shipped detail |
| `docs/decisions/` | Why a settled design is the way it is | New decisions |
| `docs/audits/` | Point-in-time investigations | New audits |
| `docs/handoffs/` | State carried between sessions of multi-session work | See below |
| `CLAUDE.md` | Workflow, gates, failure patterns | Only when one of those changes |

**Read a decision record before revisiting a settled decision, and re-verify any live figure in an audit before reusing it.**
Why: a decision's reasoning is not in the code, and an audit's numbers were true only when it was written.

**File a handoff at `docs/handoffs/<topic>-<date>-handoff.md`, and let each one fully supersede the previous one.**
Why: a new session should need only the epic's roadmap sequence and the newest handoff.

**A handoff carries only live state: live numbers with their verification time, the deploy pin, in-flight partial state, open questions with the evidence so far, the version table since the last handoff, and pointers.**
Why: that is exactly the state that goes stale between sessions and has no other home.

**Sequence, design and method notes never go in a handoff; they go in the roadmap, the decision record and this file.**
Why: anything left in a handoff chain disappears when the chain is superseded.

**Mark a superseded handoff at its top with where each surviving section now lives, and move it to `docs/handoffs/archive/`.**
Why: the chain stays readable as history without competing with the current snapshot.

