## Gates

**Never pipe a gate command; run it as its own command and read its exit code directly.**
Why: a pipeline takes the exit code of its last command, so a failing gate reports clean.

**Redirect deploy CLI output to a file instead of piping it, and verify the deploy independently.**
Why: a closed pipe can kill an upload mid-deploy and leave the old code live.

**When `{{BUILD_CMD}}` already runs the typecheck, it is the whole build gate; keep `{{TYPECHECK_CMD}}` for quick signal mid-iteration.**
Why: a second typecheck in the gate is a repeat, not a second check.

**Run the full suite (`TZ={{LOCAL_TZ}} {{TEST_CMD}}`) in the foreground as its own command, never backgrounded behind a polling loop.**
Why: a foreground run yields the exit code directly, while polling adds no information and leaves it unclear whether the gate settled.

**Check for leftover test-runner processes (`pgrep -fl {{TEST_RUNNER_PROC}}`) before every test launch, scoped or full.**
Why: a run stacked on a previous run's live workers contends for the same cores and looks exactly like a hang.

**Session order: implement → scoped runs (`{{SCOPED_TEST_CMD}}`) → break probes observed failing on scoped runs → restore by copy and confirm with `diff` → spec-update protocol → scoped doc contracts → one `{{BUILD_CMD}}` → one full suite on the final bytes.**
Why: the single full run on the committed bytes proves everything an earlier full run would, at a fraction of the cost.

**Restore a break probe from a copy made before the break, confirm the restore with `diff`, and never restore with `git checkout`.**
Why: checkout discards every uncommitted change in the file, not just the break, and an unconfirmed restore may be incomplete.

**A change touching any non-Markdown file, scripts included, owes the full suite; only an all-Markdown change runs just the doc contracts.**
Why: a script nobody deploys still runs through the same tests, and a docs-only gate on a code change is how a red test reaches main.

**A probe or new test proves nothing until it has been seen to change behavior, failing against a deliberate break.**
Why: a check that passes against its own poison is a statement about the check, not the code.

**Run the suite locally under one timezone, `{{LOCAL_TZ}}`; when a change touches date, timezone or calendar-day logic, also run the target files, not the suite, under `{{SECOND_TZ}}`.**
Why: the second leg is cheap for a handful of files and is the only per-change coverage of the other side of midnight.

**Find timezone-sensitive tests by re-running the recorded grep (`{{TZ_TEST_GREP}}`), never by trusting a saved list.**
Why: a list rots, and the grep over-matches on purpose, so its hits are a list to inspect, not a verdict.

**Prefer tests that pin their own TZ and clock, run both directions and assert the results diverged.**
Why: they stop depending on the ambient runner's timezone.

**Anchor fixture dates to the clock with a literal offset (an order created "now minus 45 days"), never a literal date that a computed cutoff compares against.**
Why: a literal date ages out of its window months later and fails on an unrelated commit; a literal offset, unlike one derived from the constant under test, keeps the test independent.

**The local gate is the primary gate; scheduled CI is a backstop that catches a skipped local gate up to a day late, so check the latest scheduled run (`{{SCHEDULED_CI_CMD}}`) at session pre-flight.**
Why: a red scheduled run means something already slipped past a local gate.

